1
00:00:00,760 --> 00:00:02,680
Welcome back.

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:06,999
Meetings can be an effective method to
distribute information, communicate with

3
00:00:06,999 --> 00:00:11,140
your team and stakeholders, and
help keep a project on track.

4
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:13,590
In this video,
you'll learn how to organize and

5
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:18,160
facilitate effective team meetings that
will lead to a successful project.

6
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:21,553
While the size, purpose, and
format of meetings may differ,

7
00:00:21,553 --> 00:00:25,710
effective meetings always have
the following elements in common.

8
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:29,840
They're structured, intentional,
collaborative, and inclusive.

9
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:32,470
Let's study these elements in more detail.

10
00:00:32,470 --> 00:00:35,170
First, effective meetings are structured.

11
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:37,280
This means they start and end on time.

12
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:39,620
The attendees have been
carefully selected.

13
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:41,441
The meeting topics are prioritized.

14
00:00:41,441 --> 00:00:44,420
And the designated note
taker has been assigned.

15
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:45,922
Managing the meeting time and

16
00:00:45,922 --> 00:00:50,150
audience lets participants know that
they're valued and appreciated.

17
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:53,050
Remember that everyone's time is valuable.

18
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:57,250
If someone's presence is unnecessary, you
don't need to invite them to the meeting.

19
00:00:57,250 --> 00:00:58,852
Maybe you can share the notes.

20
00:00:58,852 --> 00:01:01,783
Include people who can
contribute to the discussion and

21
00:01:01,783 --> 00:01:05,710
anyone who will be directly
affected by the topics discussed.

22
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:09,820
Structured meetings also have
an agenda with prioritized topics.

23
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:13,214
So the most important items
are given appropriate attention.

24
00:01:13,214 --> 00:01:16,071
It's good to think about and
set expectations for

25
00:01:16,071 --> 00:01:20,060
how long you expect the group
to spend on a given topic.

26
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:24,344
This is defined as timeboxing, which just
means you're setting a time limit for

27
00:01:24,344 --> 00:01:25,100
discussion.

28
00:01:26,310 --> 00:01:30,507
In a timebox, you give each topic a buffer
of a few minutes to make sure you're not

29
00:01:30,507 --> 00:01:32,840
over packing your agenda.

30
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:36,558
Keep the meeting time in mind and
make sure you schedule enough time for

31
00:01:36,558 --> 00:01:39,060
the topic you need to cover.

32
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:43,868
If there are several topics you need to
address consider scheduling a few shorter

33
00:01:43,868 --> 00:01:46,992
meetings with just one or
two topics as their focus.

34
00:01:46,992 --> 00:01:51,865
After your first few team meetings, think
about the pacing to know if you need to

35
00:01:51,865 --> 00:01:54,992
be more generous with the time
given to each topic or

36
00:01:54,992 --> 00:01:58,190
if the group has time for a little more.

37
00:01:58,190 --> 00:02:00,507
If you get through your topics
before your time is up,

38
00:02:00,507 --> 00:02:02,840
don't be afraid to call a meeting early.

39
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,110
Everybody loves having their time back.

40
00:02:05,110 --> 00:02:08,713
Finally, a structured meeting should
have a designated note taker.

41
00:02:08,713 --> 00:02:12,018
So you or anyone else can refer
to the meeting notes later and

42
00:02:12,018 --> 00:02:14,560
find out what was discussed.

43
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:18,690
The note taker could be you or
another meeting attendee.

44
00:02:18,690 --> 00:02:21,384
For this reason be clear about how,
when, and

45
00:02:21,384 --> 00:02:23,880
to whom the meetings outcomes are shared.

46
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:28,647
Again, this all depends on the type
of meeting you're having and

47
00:02:28,647 --> 00:02:30,260
who is attending.

48
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:33,320
I'll loop back to describe some
different types of project meetings in

49
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:34,910
the next video.

50
00:02:34,910 --> 00:02:39,710
The next point to consider is that
effective meetings are also intentional.

51
00:02:39,710 --> 00:02:43,164
This means they have a clearly
stated purpose and expectations,

52
00:02:43,164 --> 00:02:47,270
which should be in the meeting agenda
as well as the meeting invite.

53
00:02:47,270 --> 00:02:50,790
So everyone understands
why they're attending.

54
00:02:50,790 --> 00:02:53,468
The agenda needs to set
clear expectations for

55
00:02:53,468 --> 00:02:57,100
what needs to occur before and
during the meeting.

56
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:01,168
It helps attendees prepare, keeps
everyone focused on the right topics, and

57
00:03:01,168 --> 00:03:04,180
clarifies meeting expectations and goals.

58
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:08,471
The purpose of your meeting might be to
make a decision, assign tasks, propose/

59
00:03:08,471 --> 00:03:11,030
invent an idea, or something else.

60
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:14,383
Your meeting's purpose, or goal,
describes the reason you're meeting and

61
00:03:14,383 --> 00:03:16,540
what you'd like to achieve.

62
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:20,533
A well designed agenda increases the
group's ability to address problems and

63
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:22,260
prevents wasting time.

64
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:25,482
For example, you may be reviewing
last month's business sales and

65
00:03:25,482 --> 00:03:27,710
setting goals for the next month.

66
00:03:27,710 --> 00:03:31,400
Maybe your group needs to choose
coordinators for an upcoming event.

67
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:35,509
Or your goal could be to have your
supervisor prioritize a list of tasks.

68
00:03:35,509 --> 00:03:40,420
The purpose might change from meeting
to meeting, or it could stay the same.

69
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:44,787
If input is needed by attendees, be sure
to send any pre-reading materials in

70
00:03:44,787 --> 00:03:49,810
advance of the meeting so that everyone
shows up prepared to participate.

71
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:54,039
Depending on the purpose meetings
can be formal or informal,

72
00:03:54,039 --> 00:03:58,936
have as few as two or three group
members or have hundreds of attendees.

73
00:03:58,936 --> 00:04:02,632
Whatever it is, make sure you state
a clear thoughtful purpose and

74
00:04:02,632 --> 00:04:06,760
strive to achieve that purpose
by the end of the allotted time.

75
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:10,660
Another element of effective meetings is
encouraging a collaborative environment.

76
00:04:11,675 --> 00:04:16,325
Collaboration is when people work
together to produce or create something.

77
00:04:16,325 --> 00:04:19,089
There are lots of ways to make
your meetings collaborative,

78
00:04:19,089 --> 00:04:20,225
even if the purpose is for

79
00:04:20,225 --> 00:04:23,955
one person to share important
information with the rest of the group.

80
00:04:23,955 --> 00:04:26,694
One easy way to do this
is to be sure the agenda

81
00:04:26,694 --> 00:04:31,075
isn’t just full of presentations
where participants are talked at.

82
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:36,540
Be sure the agenda is clear about
the objectives of the session.

83
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:41,071
Will a decision be made, or is the session
for information and discussion?

84
00:04:41,071 --> 00:04:45,869
Second, have a digital shared meeting
document and encourage participants to

85
00:04:45,869 --> 00:04:49,960
write any comments or
thoughts directly in the document.

86
00:04:49,960 --> 00:04:53,840
But more importantly,
remind them that the notes will be shared.

87
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:55,771
This encourages active listening and

88
00:04:55,771 --> 00:04:58,810
participation in real
time during the meeting.

89
00:04:58,810 --> 00:05:03,194
Last, it's important to respect and
embrace each individual's preferred

90
00:05:03,194 --> 00:05:08,410
communication style even in meetings,
especially in collaborative meetings.

91
00:05:08,410 --> 00:05:12,148
Let folks know they're welcome to
respond verbally, through the chat,

92
00:05:12,148 --> 00:05:16,230
in the meeting notes, or
in any other format you'd like to include.

93
00:05:16,230 --> 00:05:19,970
The final element of an effective
meeting is that it's inclusive.

94
00:05:19,970 --> 00:05:21,928
Inclusivity is the practice or

95
00:05:21,928 --> 00:05:27,250
policy of including people who might
otherwise be excluded or marginalized.

96
00:05:27,250 --> 00:05:30,767
When it's done well, inclusion
leads to excellent collaboration,

97
00:05:30,767 --> 00:05:34,700
ensuring that every participants
contributions are valued.

98
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:38,859
As a project manager, you play a critical
role in ensuring that meeting attendees

99
00:05:38,859 --> 00:05:41,070
feel supported and included.

100
00:05:41,070 --> 00:05:44,920
So offering additional resources for
ways to participate is helpful.

101
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:49,532
For both in-person and virtual meetings,
consider appointing a meeting moderator or

102
00:05:49,532 --> 00:05:51,040
facilitator.

103
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:54,793
This person will guide the meeting and
help participants ask questions in

104
00:05:54,793 --> 00:05:57,390
real-time, while someone
else is presenting.

105
00:05:58,820 --> 00:06:02,760
This way the presenter can stay focused
on their topic while the moderator pays

106
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:04,492
attention to the participants and

107
00:06:04,492 --> 00:06:07,250
can help guide participants
on when they can chime in.

108
00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:10,994
It's also a good idea to leave
space in the meeting for

109
00:06:10,994 --> 00:06:12,816
participants who've been quiet.

110
00:06:12,816 --> 00:06:15,546
At the close of a topic or
before the meeting ends,

111
00:06:15,546 --> 00:06:18,731
you might consider going around
the room once in a while and

112
00:06:18,731 --> 00:06:23,050
asking each person to comment on
a particular issue or question.

113
00:06:23,050 --> 00:06:26,528
I like to call this a final flight
check before everyone takes off and

114
00:06:26,528 --> 00:06:28,510
goes their separate ways.

115
00:06:28,510 --> 00:06:31,883
A key component of inclusivity is
ensuring that your meetings and

116
00:06:31,883 --> 00:06:34,210
presentations are accessible.

117
00:06:34,210 --> 00:06:37,880
You'll find more information on
this in the course resources.

118
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:41,916
Another thing to consider is the level of
internet access when conducting virtual

119
00:06:41,916 --> 00:06:43,240
meetings.

120
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:48,260
For those without strong internet access,
provide a phone dialing option.

121
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:53,160
Most online meeting tools, including
Google Meet and Zoom, have this option.

122
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:56,864
Let participants know it's okay to
turn off their cameras if they need to

123
00:06:56,864 --> 00:07:00,085
improve the connection or
adjust the video quality.

124
00:07:00,085 --> 00:07:03,211
Focusing on inclusion helps
build a sense of belonging and

125
00:07:03,211 --> 00:07:08,285
serves as a reminder that we live in a
world with many different kinds of people.

126
00:07:08,285 --> 00:07:12,692
Creating a meeting space where people
have different views, backgrounds, and

127
00:07:12,692 --> 00:07:16,535
experiences can do their best work and
be there for one another is key.

128
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:22,352
These tips aren't exhaustive by any means,
but hopefully they can be useful to

129
00:07:22,352 --> 00:07:27,290
start empowering others to
meaningfully join in and contribute.

130
00:07:27,290 --> 00:07:31,540
When done right, meetings can be
incredibly useful communication tools.

131
00:07:31,540 --> 00:07:34,903
That's why it's important to be
purposeful when designing meetings

132
00:07:34,903 --> 00:07:38,390
that are structured, intentional,
collaborative, and inclusive.

133
00:07:39,470 --> 00:07:42,850
This will earn you a reputation for
writing efficient, inclusive,

134
00:07:42,850 --> 00:07:46,580
successful meetings where everyone
feels appreciated, which is great for

135
00:07:46,580 --> 00:07:48,550
your project and your career.

136
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:49,308
Great job, so

137
00:07:49,308 --> 00:07:53,484
now we know a bit more about the effective
methods to distribute information,

138
00:07:53,484 --> 00:07:58,370
communicate with your team and
stakeholders, and keep a project on track.

139
00:07:58,370 --> 00:08:02,542
We've also learned some of the elements
of creating an effective meeting like

140
00:08:02,542 --> 00:08:06,790
including a clear agenda, attendee list,
taking detailed notes, and more.

141
00:08:08,150 --> 00:08:12,630
In the next video, we'll learn all about
the most common types of project meetings.
